window.docContents[127].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Programming___-Proving___-and-Performance--Proving-Equivalence--Exercise--Warming-Up":{"contents":"Write your own proofs for , , and  using the induction tactic.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance\u0009Proving Equivalence\u0009Exercise","header":"8.2.4.1. Warming Up","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Programming___-Proving___-and-Performance--Proving-Equivalence--Exercise--Warming-Up"},"/#loops":{"contents":"Just as every program with mutable state can be rewritten to a program that passes the state as arguments, every loop can be rewritten as a recursive function.\nFrom one perspective,  is most clear as a recursive function.\nAfter all, its definition mirrors the structure of the list: if the head passes the check, then it should be returned; otherwise look in the tail.\nWhen no more entries remain, the answer is .\nFrom another perspective,  is most clear as a loop.\nAfter all, the program consults the entries in order until a satisfactory one is found, at which point it terminates.\nIf the loop terminates without having returned, the answer is .\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monad Transformers\u0009More do Features","header":"6.4.3. Loops","id":"/#loops"},"/#projecting-rows":{"contents":"Given evidence that  is a subschema of , a row in  can be projected into a row in .\nThis is done using the evidence that  is a subschema of , which explains where each column of  is found in .\nThe new row in  is built up one column at a time by retrieving the value from the appropriate place in the old row.\n\nThe function that performs this projection, , has three cases, one for each case of  itself.\nIt uses  together with each  in the  argument to construct the projected row:\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming with Dependent Types\u0009Worked Example: Typed Queries\u0009Schemas and Tables","header":"7.3.2.4. Projecting Rows","id":"/#projecting-rows"},"/#typed-queries-error-messages":{"contents":"Many potential errors are ruled out by the definition of .\nFor instance, forgetting the added qualifier in  yields a compile-time error that highlights the column reference :\n\n\n\nThis is excellent feedback!\nOn the other hand, the text of the error message is quite difficult to act on:\n\n\n\nSimilarly, forgetting to add prefixes to the names of the two tables results in an error on by decide, which should provide evidence that the schemas are in fact disjoint:\n\n\n\nThis error message is more helpful:\n\n\n\nLean's macro system contains everything needed not only to provide a convenient syntax for queries, but also to arrange for the error messages to be helpful.\nUnfortunately, it is beyond the scope of this book to provide a description of implementing languages with Lean macros.\nAn indexed family such as  is probably best as the core of a typed database interaction library, rather than its user interface.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming with Dependent Types\u0009Worked Example: Typed Queries\u0009Executing Queries","header":"7.3.5.6. Errors You May Meet","id":"/#typed-queries-error-messages"}});